                subject: "Stop pinning stdout/stderr interleaving in the spawn runner test",
                details: "The test asserted one merged ordering across two separate pipes, which\nis a bet no OS ever promised to pay out. stdout and stderr are\nindependently buffered and delivered to the parent process, so which\nline lands first between the two streams is scheduling, not code — it\npassed on whichever machine wrote it and lost the coin flip on Linux\nCI. Reading process.ts confirms the runner itself is innocent: two\nseparate readline interfaces, one per stream, each emitting its own\nlines the instant they arrive, with zero batching and zero cross-\nstream coordination to blame. So the fix stays in the test: keep every\nline captured exactly once, keep each line's stream tag pinned, keep\norder preserved within each stream (that part IS guaranteed and losing\nit would be a real regression) — just stop grading the shuffle between\ntwo pipes nobody synchronized.\n\n呢個測試將兩條唔同水喉嘅出水次序,當成一條水喉嚟計較,執輸都唔出奇。\nstdout 同 stderr 係兩條獨立喉,邊條快邊條慢係作業系統話事,唔關\nprocess.ts 事——讀完個 runner 先發現佢好無辜,兩個 readline 各睇各\n條喉,行到即刻出,冇偷步冇夾埋一齊出,乜嘢跨喉次序都冧唔到佢。所以\n呢次唔改代碼,淨係改測試個斷言:每行都要收到、唔可以少收多收、標籤\n要啱,同一條喉入面嘅次序仲係要企得住——呢啲先係真係有保證嘅嘢,冇\n理由撤走。淨係唔再賭兩條喉邊條行先。",
