                subject: "Add a git-repository world source: publish and sync a world incrementally",
                details: "A Minecraft world can now live in a git repository and be kept up to date\nwithout re-zipping or re-uploading unchanged region files. WorldRepoHost\nreuses pages/hosting.ts's proven publish shape (git directory kept outside\nthe payload, a marker file that guards against clobbering a branch this\ntool did not write, batched staging, a push read back from GitHub rather\nthan assumed) and shows why an orphan-per-sync commit is still the right\ndesign for a world: the remote never accumulates history, and the push\nstill only sends what changed because git excludes every object reachable\nfrom a ref the remote currently advertises, independent of whether the new\ncommit is a child of the old one - as long as the local object database\nstill knows those objects, which staying in the same git directory across\nsyncs guarantees. One addition over the map case: if the remote branch\nalready exists and this computer has never synced it, the objects are\nfetched in first so that exclusion has something to exclude against.\n\nExposes remoteTip() - one cheap gh api call for a branch's current commit,\nno clone, no download - for a scheduled-render lane's change detection to\ncompare against without touching the world at all.\n\ngit 依家識做世界嘅家 - 唔使成日拉大隻 zip 上去，淨係送啱啱改嗰幾舊 region\n檔案。抄 Pages 果套「隔離嘅 .git、標記file 睇門口、每次都覆頭來過」嘅橋，\n但今次覆頭來過都仲係慳流量,因為 git send 之前，會照做識做嘅嘢：睇下 remote\n度已經有咩,冇嘅先send。仲加多一味:第一次同一個 target 對 sync,會先去攞\n返 remote 現有嘅嘢返嚟,咁樣先至夠料慳到嘢。",
