                subject: "Test the tour's reduced-motion path directly, not just claim it in a comment",
                details: "Two behavioural checks rather than a fragile CSS-string match: with the\nplatform's own prefers-reduced-motion media query answering true, every\nscrollIntoView the overlay fires while bringing a step's anchor into view\nnow has to carry a non-smooth behaviour; with it answering false, at least\none call has to ask for \"smooth\" explicitly, so the two cases are proven\nto actually differ rather than both quietly doing the same thing. A third\nattempt at asserting the disabled CSS transition via document.querySelectorAll\n(\"style\") turned out to depend on exactly how this Vite/Vitest setup injects\nan SFC's <style> block into jsdom, which is implementation detail this\npackage does not otherwise couple its tests to - dropped in favour of the\ntwo that check real, observable behaviour.\n\n直接測試導覽嘅減少動態路徑,唔淨係喺註解度講吓\n\n用兩個真正嘅行為檢查,取代一個好脆弱嘅 CSS 字串比對:當平台自己嘅\nprefers-reduced-motion 媒體查詢答「係」,導覽帶住個步驟嘅錨點入畫嗰陣每一次\nscrollIntoView 而家都一定要用非平滑嘅行為;答「唔係」嗰陣,至少有一次要\n明確攞「smooth」,咁樣先證明到兩種情況真係唔一樣,唔係兩個都靜靜雞做緊\n同一件事。第三個諗住用 document.querySelectorAll(\"style\") 去斷言個 CSS\ntransition 熄咗嘅寫法,原來要睇呢個 Vite/Vitest 環境實際點樣將 SFC 嘅\n<style> 塊注入 jsdom,呢種實作細節呢個 package 平時嘅測試都唔會咁樣綁死,\n所以擺低咗,淨係留低嗰兩個真正檢查得到行為嘅測試。",
                category: "interface",
